  "slug": "cross-power-spectrum-b133",
  "summary": "Five preprints use simulations to test or replace costly modelling: U-Net emulators, w0 mappings for dynamical dark energy, and a null test for assembly bias.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is using simulations and mocks as validation targets for cheaper or more general modelling of cosmological observables. Two papers use 3D U-Nets as field-level emulators: one maps density and velocity fields to Lyman-alpha flux, reproducing P1D, bispectrum and trispectrum across IllustrisTNG and FLAMINGO, and one predicts hot gas temperature and electron density from dark matter in CAMELS IllustrisTNG. Two papers show that N-body dynamics depend only on the expansion history a(τ), so constant-w simulations can reproduce CPL and general w(z) models, with power spectra agreeing within 0.2% in one case. A fifth proposes luminosity-weighted marked correlations as a null test for assembly bias using DESI-BGS mocks. New relative to the prior synthesis is the focus on field-level emulation and equivalence mapping rather than EFT or EBL cross-correlation forecasts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
